{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>All the homeborn in Israel shall dwell in booths.</b> He specified precisely 'homeborn' (ezrach), which is the language of 'settled resident' (toshav), because at the time of gathering produce from the field, every person wants to go from the field into his house to dwell in it with permanent settledness, and the Torah was concerned lest through permanent dwelling his heart grow haughty, for his hand has found plenty, and he grow fat and kick. Therefore it says 'all the homeborn' — whoever wishes to be like a settled resident in this world and not like a stranger, to him did HaShem command to go forth from a permanent dwelling to a temporary dwelling, so that he recognize his lowly worth, that in this world he is but a stranger and not a settled resident, like one who lodges among lodgers; and through this he will not trust in the shadow of his roof-beams but will trust in the shadow of Shaddai — as Israel did when they went forth from Egypt, that they did not dwell in paneled and distinguished houses, but within the envelopment of seven clouds in which the glory of YHWH was among them. And this is 'in the shadow of Shaddai' (בְּצֵל שַׁדַּי) and not in the shadow of the cedar beams of their houses. And this is what it says: ״כִּי בַסֻּכּוֹת הוֹשַׁבְתִּי אֶת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ and so forth — for one cannot say that it speaks of ordinary booths of wayfarers, for if so it should have said ״כִּי בַסֻּכּוֹת יָשְׁבוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״; what is the force of ״הוֹשַׁבְתִּי״ ('I made [them] dwell')? This implies that the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself and in His glory seated them in the booths — and this can be none other than the envelopment of seven clouds, which YHWH spread as a covering over them. For therefore they came 'in the shadow of Shaddai,' blessed be He.'"], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The closing quotation in the source begins with the word למסך (as a covering/masking), which is unusual; rendered hyper-literally as 'spread as a covering.' The final phrase reads 'they came in the shadow of Shaddai,' playing on בצל שדי (Psalms 91:1) throughout the comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:22.511079730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}